# mito-demand — the motor needs BOTH (mito-queue's real lesson)
#
# mito-fuel put glucose on the front, but its pump→reservoir→ATP was a straight
# line: more fuel, more output, always. The real machine has a second demand.
# ATP synthase is a TWO-substrate motor: it needs a proton from the gradient AND
# an ADP to charge. Flood it with protons but starve it of ADP and it stalls just
# as dead as if the gradient were flat. That is an AND-gate, and an AND-gate is
# the one thing a single edge cannot say — an edge reads one node. So the motor
# is a `reaction`, reading BOTH its inputs at once, drawn as one spinning hub:
#
#     reservoir + adp -> atp : limiting(k) @ rotor
#
# `limiting` is the bottleneck: the wheel turns at the pace of the SCARCER feed
# (k · min(protons, adp)). Either input dry → min → 0 → the motor stops.
#
# Two dials feed the motor's two sides:
#   glucose  sets the proton pump        (etc  -> reservoir)   — the gradient side
#   demand   refills the ADP queue       (load -> adp)         — the substrate side
# ADP and ATP are the cell's energy tokens: the motor charges adp→atp, and the
# cell's WORK spends atp and hands the empty token back (so `demand` — how hard
# the cell is working — is exactly the rate ADP is regenerated). More demand,
# more empty tokens waiting; cut demand and the ADP queue starves the motor even
# with a brim-full dam. (We supply ADP from an external `load` rather than a
# strictly conserved atp↔adp loop — the same simplification mito-queue makes —
# so an empty pool can't mint tokens from nothing.)
#
# The four corners (mito-queue's note, now falling out of the math):
#   fuel high, demand zero  → ADP queue empties → motor stalls, the dam floods to 100
#   demand high, fuel zero  → dam drains → motor stalls, ADP piles up
#   both high               → the wheel runs flat out, limited by whichever you pinch
#   both low                → it idles
#
# THIS is the model that forced `@ rotor` onto a reaction: a hub where two feeds
# converge and one product leaves, spinning at the shared rate.

node glucose   : range(0, 100), init(60)                         # the fuel dial — the proton pump
node demand    : range(0, 100), init(60)                         # the demand dial — how hard the cell works
node reservoir : range(0, 100), init(15) @ bucket(fill = dots)   # the proton dam — the gradient
node adp       : range(0, 100), init(40) @ bucket(fill = dots)   # the ADP queue — empty tokens
node atp       : range(0, 100), init(10) @ bucket(fill = dots)   # ATP — charged tokens
external etc        # electron transport chain — the pump (glucose-derived carriers drive it)
external load       # the cell's work, handing back spent tokens as ADP
external work       # charged ATP, spent

etc  -> reservoir : proportional(of = glucose, k = 0.10)         # pump rate SET by glucose
load -> adp       : proportional(of = demand,  k = 0.10)         # demand refills the ADP queue (matched to the pump)
atp  -> work      : proportional(k = 0.15)                       # ATP spent (of defaults to atp — only what you have)

# the motor: one proton + one ADP -> one ATP, at the pace of the scarcer feed.
pathway motor : reservoir + adp -> atp : limiting(k = 0.4) @ rotor
